---
import rules from '../data/rules.json'

/**
 * Numbered pillars that cite live catalogue counts. Kept out of the MDX so the
 * counts stay tied to `rules.json` without templating noise in the prose file.
 */
const total = rules.length
const staticRules = rules.filter((rule) => !rule.needs_model).length
const modelRules = total - staticRules
const fixable = rules.filter((rule) => rule.fixable).length
---

<ol class="signal-list">
  <li>
    <span class="idx">01</span>
    <div>
      <h3>Static first</h3>
      <p>
        {staticRules} of {total} rules never touch a model. No network, no tokens, no waiting — the
        whole catalogue runs in milliseconds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="idx">02</span>
    <div>
      <h3>Every finding cites a source</h3>
      <p>
        A linter that says “wrong” without saying who says so gets argued with. Every rule names the
        document its claim comes from — including yours, in a plugin.
      </p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="idx">03</span>
    <div>
      <h3>Fixes are computed</h3>
      <p>
        {fixable} rules carry a fix that is calculated rather than written. Path separators,
        executable bits, contents lists — a model never edits your files.
      </p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="idx">04</span>
    <div>
      <h3>It says what it did not do</h3>
      <p>
        {modelRules} rules need a reader. Without a provider, the report names them as skipped. A
        review that quietly missed half its work must never read like a clean one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </li>
</ol>
